preConditionHook:
// The classic floor-trader daily pivot, (priorDayHigh + priorDayLow +
// priorDayClose) / 3, tested for PROXIMITY: the close is within
// toleranceTicks ticks of it. Identical both sides -- a magnet is a magnet
// from either direction.
//
// Brooks writes only "daily pivots" and names no formula, so the classic
// (H + L + C) / 3 is used: the one everyone means by the bare phrase, and the
// only one of the three families (classic / Woodie / Camarilla) that needs no
// extra weighting choice. R1/S1 and the wider bands are deliberately not
// carried; they would be a ratio input on top of this level.
//
// prevHigh / prevLow / prevClose ARE HighD(1) / LowD(1) / CloseD(1), which the
// EasyLanguage twin calls and the engine does not expose. MEASURED (the
// highd/lowd register row): the day is the wall-clock CALENDAR date, the SAME
// boundary as date <> date[1] -- one slicing serves both -- and the functions
// return a -1 SENTINEL while fewer than that many complete days exist. The
// mirror therefore keeps the running day extremes, rolls them at the date
// change, and keeps -1 as the initial value so the guard below means the same
// thing in both twins. The guard is load-bearing: -1 as a price would drag the
// pivot far under every real price.
//
// WARM-UP, THE ONE PLACE THE TWINS CAN DIFFER: TradeStation's HighD reads the
// chart's own data and answers on the very first evaluated bar, while this
// mirror can only see bars from Max Bars Back on. sawStart makes that honest
// rather than silent -- a day is published as "the prior session" only once
// its own first bar has been observed, so the three prior-session values hold
// the sentinel through the first partial day AND the first fully observed day,
// and the rule is simply silent there. From the second date change on the two
// agree exactly. All three roll together so the pivot is never mixed from two
// different days; CloseD(1) alone would be exact one day earlier.
//
// On a daily or 1440-minute series the date changes on every bar, so the
// running extremes collapse to high[1] / low[1] / close[1] and the pivot is
// the trivial prior-bar expression -- the rule is correct on both timeframes.
//
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, VERIFIED and per-symbol constant
// for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++ reads
// token for token against its EasyLanguage.
//
// Not CloseWithinAtrOfVwap, which tracks the CURRENT session's volume-weighted
// price and scales its band by ATR: this reads three fixed values from the
// COMPLETED prior session and bands them in ticks.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the
// generator only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a
// redefinition when this rule is placed twice in one strategy.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
if (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1))) {
    if (sawStart) {
        prevHigh = curHigh;
        prevLow = curLow;
        prevClose = close[1];
    }
    sawStart = true;
    curHigh = high[0];
    curLow = low[0];
} else {
    if (el_gt(high[0], curHigh)) curHigh = high[0];
    if (el_lt(low[0], curLow)) curLow = low[0];
}
pivot = (prevHigh + prevLow + prevClose) / 3.0;
nearPivot = !el_eq(prevHigh, -1.0) && !el_eq(prevLow, -1.0) && !el_eq(prevClose, -1.0)
    && el_le(std::fabs(close[0] - pivot), toleranceTicks * tickSize);
